# Themis network edge (VM-NET-1, EDR-GUI-01 D3): TLS in front of the dashboard, and the two API
# routes a build system needs, on 443 and nothing else.
#
# Every node listens on loopback (INSTALLATION.md §7a step 1). Caddy terminates HTTPS and
# publishes exactly four things: the dashboard (default), Evidence's intake under /evidence/
# (a CI job uploads the new SBOM there with its product-scoped key), the Registry under
# /registry/ (the same job finds-or-creates its project with the same key, confined to its own
# product — EDR-SECURITY-01 D4), and Communication under /communication/ (the CI job's callback,
# delivery:callback key). handle_path strips the prefix, so the nodes see their own /api/v1/...
# paths. Governance, Knowledge and Intelligence are NOT published: scripts run on the host, and a
# remote caller for one of them is the moment to add a block here, deliberately. reverse_proxy adds X-Forwarded-Proto, so the dashboard's
# session cookie turns Secure.
#
# Install: copy to /etc/caddy/Caddyfile and replace the site names with the VM's own: the full
# name (`hostname -f`), the short name (`hostname -s`) and its address (`hostname -I`), because
# Caddy only completes a TLS handshake for a name it holds a certificate for — a browser typing
# the short name against a Caddyfile that lists only the full one gets ERR_SSL_PROTOCOL_ERROR
# (measured 2026-10-19). `tls internal` issues a certificate from Caddy's local CA — browsers and the
# CI host must trust that CA (`caddy trust` on the VM, or import
# /var/lib/caddy/.local/share/caddy/pki/authorities/local/root.crt), or replace the line with
# `tls /etc/ssl/certs/themis.crt /etc/ssl/private/themis.key` for a company certificate. For a
# public name, delete the line and Caddy obtains one from Let's Encrypt.

themis.example.internal, themis, 10.0.0.10 {
	tls internal
	encode gzip
	handle_path /evidence/* {
		reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:8081
	}
	handle_path /registry/* {
		reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:8082
	}
	handle_path /communication/* {
		reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:8084
	}
	handle {
		reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:8090
	}
}
